.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt DTFL_EVENT_LINK_REPLAY 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_event_link_replay
.Nd replay a captured event stream into a scope
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/timely_ops.h
.Ft dtfl_stream *
.Fn dtfl_event_link_replay "dtfl_event_link *l" "dtfl_scope *scope"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn dtfl_event_link_replay
reconstructs, as a stream in
.Fa scope ,
the stream whose events were captured into
.Fa l .
A
.Em Progress
event advances the replay operator's capabilities; a
.Em Messages
event is re-emitted at its recorded time.
The replayed stream carries the same records and the same frontier the
captured stream had, so a downstream operator sees the computation as if
it had been built in this scope.
.Pp
The scope's time class must be the class the link was created with.
The operator re-activates itself after each schedule so that a link
still being filled by a running capture keeps draining.
.Pp
The link is not consumed: it may be extracted after replay, and a
thread-safe link
.Pq Xr dtfl_event_link_new_sync 3
may be replayed while it is still being pushed from another thread.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Returns the replayed stream, owned by
.Fa scope .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtfl_stream *s = dtfl_event_link_replay(link, scope);
dtfl_stream_inspect(s, print_record, DTFL_NOCTX);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
This function does not return error codes.
A scope whose time class differs from the link's is a programming error
and calls the panic hook.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_event_link_extract 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_execute 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_stream_capture 3 ,
.Xr dtfl 7
.Sh HISTORY
This is the C form of timely's
.Fn replay_into
operator of
.Pa capture/replay.rs .
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
The replayed stream and its operator belong to the worker thread that
built them.
The link may be shared across threads only when it was created with
.Fn dtfl_event_link_new_sync .
